---
title: Searchable encrypted media
description: How NeoWorks makes end-to-end encrypted media searchable — client-side embeddings, the plaintext-vector tradeoff, and why the server cannot do it.
---

Search over media (e.g. "find the beach photo") needs an embedding of the media's *content*.
But media is [end-to-end encrypted](/docs/neoworks/encryption) — the server only holds
ciphertext. This page documents how NeoWorks reconciles the two and the tradeoff it accepts.

## Why the server can't embed media

The text/[memory](/docs/neoworks/openschema) search pipeline embeds content **server-side**: a
backfill worker reads the plaintext and computes a vector. That is impossible for media — the
server cannot decrypt it, by design. So a server-side backfill worker for media **does not
exist**.

## Decision: embed on the client, store the vector as queryable data

> Media is embedded **in the browser**, after the Vault decrypts it; the resulting vector is
> uploaded and stored **as plaintext (queryable) data** so the server can run vector + keyword
> search over it.

Flow:

1. On upload, the server records the media as having a **pending** embedding (images only).
2. The client lists pending items, downloads + decrypts each through the
   [Vault](/docs/neoworks/encryption#the-vault-a-cross-origin-crypto-sandbox), and runs an
   in-browser image model to produce a vector.
3. The client uploads the raw vector; the server marks the embedding **ready** and indexes it.
4. Search embeds the (plaintext) query text server-side and runs a hybrid
   vector + keyword search, fused with reciprocal rank fusion.

### The tradeoff

Storing the vector as plaintext means the server (and anyone who can read that table) learns
**semantic information** about an otherwise end-to-end-encrypted image — roughly "what it's of."
This is a deliberate, scoped relaxation, consistent with the platform rule that **queryable
data is not E2E**: opting an image into search opts its embedding out of E2E. The image *bytes*
remain end-to-end encrypted; only a derived vector becomes queryable.

The alternative — encrypting the vectors — was rejected because it makes server-side
nearest-neighbour search impossible (the server would have to ship all vectors to the client to
search locally).

## One embedding space for text and images

NeoWorks standardizes embeddings on **Nomic** at **768 dimensions** (Apache-2.0 licensed):

- **Images** are embedded in the browser with `nomic-embed-vision-v1.5`.
- **Text queries** (and memory text) are embedded with `nomic-embed-text-v1.5`.

The two models are aligned into the **same 768-d space**, so a typed text query retrieves
relevant images directly — without a separate cross-modal step. The same space is shared with
[memory](/docs/neoworks/openschema) search, leaving room for unified search across corpora.

> A non-aligned or non-commercially-licensed model (e.g. a CLIP variant under a research-only
> license) was rejected: the requirement was an open license **and** a vision encoder aligned to
> the text encoder.

## Consequences

- Embedding is **best-effort and asynchronous**. Media is fully usable before its vector exists;
  keyword (filename) search and metadata filters work immediately.
- Because only the client can embed media, an item stays **pending** until a capable device
  opens the app — search simply misses un-embedded items until then.
